Task 1: Install and Configure the Multus CNI Plugin
Subtask 1.1: Verify Current CNI Configuration
First, let's examine the current networking setup in your OpenShift cluster.

Connect to your lab environment and open a terminal session.

Check the current CNI configuration:

# List current network attachment definitions
oc get network-attachment-definitions -A

# Check the cluster network configuration
oc get network.config.openshift.io cluster -o yaml
Verify the Multus CNI is available:
# Check if Multus is already installed (it should be in OpenShift 4.x)
oc get pods -n openshift-multus

# Verify Multus daemonset
oc get daemonset -n openshift-multus
Subtask 1.2: Create Additional Network Configurations
Now we'll create additional network configurations that our VMs can use.

Create a bridge network configuration:
# Create a file for the bridge network
cat << EOF > bridge-network.yaml
apiVersion: k8s.cni.cncf.io/v1
kind: NetworkAttachmentDefinition
metadata:
  name: bridge-network
  namespace: default
spec:
  config: |
    {
      "cniVersion": "0.3.1",
      "name": "bridge-network",
      "type": "bridge",
      "bridge": "br1",
      "isDefaultGateway": false,
      "forceAddress": false,
      "ipMasq": true,
      "hairpinMode": true,
      "ipam": {
        "type": "host-local",
        "subnet": "192.168.100.0/24",
        "rangeStart": "192.168.100.10",
        "rangeEnd": "192.168.100.100",
        "gateway": "192.168.100.1"
      }
    }
EOF

# Apply the bridge network configuration
oc apply -f bridge-network.yaml
Create a macvlan network configuration:
# Create a file for the macvlan network
cat << EOF > macvlan-network.yaml
apiVersion: k8s.cni.cncf.io/v1
kind: NetworkAttachmentDefinition
metadata:
  name: macvlan-network
  namespace: default
spec:
  config: |
    {
      "cniVersion": "0.3.1",
      "name": "macvlan-network",
      "type": "macvlan",
      "master": "eth0",
      "mode": "bridge",
      "ipam": {
        "type": "host-local",
        "subnet": "192.168.200.0/24",
        "rangeStart": "192.168.200.10",
        "rangeEnd": "192.168.200.100",
        "gateway": "192.168.200.1"
      }
    }
EOF

# Apply the macvlan network configuration
oc apply -f macvlan-network.yaml
Verify the network attachment definitions:
# List all network attachment definitions
oc get network-attachment-definitions

# Get detailed information about our networks
oc describe network-attachment-definition bridge-network
oc describe network-attachment-definition macvlan-network
Subtask 1.3: Configure Node Network Preparation
Prepare the nodes for additional networking.

Create a MachineConfig for bridge setup (if needed):
cat << EOF > bridge-machineconfig.yaml
apiVersion: machineconfiguration.openshift.io/v1
kind: MachineConfig
metadata:
  labels:
    machineconfiguration.openshift.io/role: worker
  name: 99-worker-bridge-setup
spec:
  config:
    ignition:
      version: 3.2.0
    systemd:
      units:
      - name: bridge-setup.service
        enabled: true
        contents: |
          [Unit]
          Description=Setup bridge for multihoming
          After=network.target
          
          [Service]
          Type=oneshot
          ExecStart=/bin/bash -c 'ip link add br1 type bridge || true; ip link set br1 up || true'
          RemainAfterExit=yes
          
          [Install]
          WantedBy=multi-user.target
EOF

# Apply the machine config (this will trigger node reboots)
# oc apply -f bridge-machineconfig.yaml
Note: In a lab environment, we'll skip the MachineConfig application to avoid node reboots. The bridge will be created dynamically.

Task 2: Create a VM with Multiple Network Interfaces
Subtask 2.1: Prepare VM Resources
Let's create the necessary resources for our multihomed VM.

Create a namespace for our VM:
# Create a dedicated namespace
oc create namespace vm-multihoming

# Set the current context to use this namespace
oc project vm-multihoming
Create a DataVolume for the VM:
cat << EOF > vm-datavolume.yaml
apiVersion: cdi.kubevirt.io/v1beta1
kind: DataVolume
metadata:
  name: fedora-multihome-dv
  namespace: vm-multihoming
spec:
  source:
    registry:
      url: "docker://quay.io/containerdisks/fedora:latest"
  storage:
    resources:
      requests:
        storage: 10Gi
    storageClassName: gp3-csi
EOF

# Apply the DataVolume
oc apply -f vm-datavolume.yaml
Wait for the DataVolume to be ready:
# Monitor the DataVolume creation
oc get datavolume fedora-multihome-dv -w

# Check the status (wait until PHASE shows Succeeded)
oc get datavolume fedora-multihome-dv
Subtask 2.2: Create the Multihomed Virtual Machine
Now we'll create a VM with multiple network interfaces.

Create the VM definition with multiple networks:
cat << EOF > multihomed-vm.yaml
apiVersion: kubevirt.io/v1
kind: VirtualMachine
metadata:
  name: fedora-multihome
  namespace: vm-multihoming
  labels:
    app: fedora-multihome
spec:
  running: true
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        kubevirt.io/domain: fedora-multihome
      annotations:
        k8s.v1.cni.cncf.io/networks: |
          [
            {
              "name": "bridge-network",
              "namespace": "default"
            },
            {
              "name": "macvlan-network", 
              "namespace": "default"
            }
          ]
    spec:
      domain:
        cpu:
          cores: 2
        devices:
          disks:
          - disk:
              bus: virtio
            name: containerdisk
          - disk:
              bus: virtio
            name: cloudinitdisk
          interfaces:
          - name: default
            masquerade: {}
          - name: bridge-net
            bridge: {}
          - name: macvlan-net
            bridge: {}
          rng: {}
        machine:
          type: pc-q35-rhel9.2.0
        resources:
          requests:
            memory: 2Gi
      evictionStrategy: LiveMigrate
      networks:
      - name: default
        pod: {}
      - name: bridge-net
        multus:
          networkName: default/bridge-network
      - name: macvlan-net
        multus:
          networkName: default/macvlan-network
      terminationGracePeriodSeconds: 180
      volumes:
      - dataVolume:
          name: fedora-multihome-dv
        name: containerdisk
      - cloudInitNoCloud:
          userData: |
            #cloud-config
            user: fedora
            password: fedora
            chpasswd: { expire: False }
            ssh_authorized_keys:
              - ssh-rsa AAAAB3NzaC1yc2EAAAADAQABAAABgQC... # Add your SSH key here
            runcmd:
              - dnf install -y net-tools tcpdump
              - systemctl enable sshd
              - systemctl start sshd
        name: cloudinitdisk
EOF

# Apply the VM configuration
oc apply -f multihomed-vm.yaml
Monitor VM creation and startup:
# Watch the VM status
oc get vm fedora-multihome -w

# Check the VMI (Virtual Machine Instance) status
oc get vmi fedora-multihome

# Get detailed VM information
oc describe vm fedora-multihome
Subtask 2.3: Access the Virtual Machine
Let's access our VM to configure the additional network interfaces.

Connect to the VM console:
# Access the VM console
virtctl console fedora-multihome -n vm-multihoming
Login to the VM (use credentials: fedora/fedora)

Check the network interfaces inside the VM:

# List all network interfaces
ip addr show

# Check routing table
ip route show

# List network interface names
ls /sys/class/net/
You should see multiple network interfaces:

eth0: Default pod network (masquerade)
eth1: Bridge network interface
eth2: Macvlan network interface
Task 3: Verify VM Network Connectivity Across Multiple Networks
Subtask 3.1: Configure Additional Network Interfaces
Inside the VM, we need to configure the additional network interfaces.

Configure the bridge network interface (eth1):
# Configure eth1 with bridge network settings
sudo ip addr add 192.168.100.50/24 dev eth1
sudo ip link set eth1 up

# Verify the configuration
ip addr show eth1
Configure the macvlan network interface (eth2):
# Configure eth2 with macvlan network settings
sudo ip addr add 192.168.200.50/24 dev eth2
sudo ip link set eth2 up

# Verify the configuration
ip addr show eth2
Update the routing table:
# Add routes for the additional networks
sudo ip route add 192.168.100.0/24 dev eth1
sudo ip route add 192.168.200.0/24 dev eth2

# Display the updated routing table
ip route show
Subtask 3.2: Test Network Connectivity
Now let's test connectivity across all networks.

Exit the VM console (press Ctrl+] to exit virtctl console)

Create test pods on different networks to verify connectivity:

# Create a test pod on the bridge network
cat << EOF > test-pod-bridge.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: test-pod-bridge
  namespace: vm-multihoming
  annotations:
    k8s.v1.cni.cncf.io/networks: default/bridge-network
spec:
  containers:
  - name: test-container
    image: registry.access.redhat.com/ubi8/ubi:latest
    command: ["/bin/bash"]
    args: ["-c", "sleep 3600"]
    resources:
      requests:
        memory: "64Mi"
        cpu: "50m"
EOF

# Apply the test pod
oc apply -f test-pod-bridge.yaml
Create a test pod on the macvlan network:
cat << EOF > test-pod-macvlan.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: test-pod-macvlan
  namespace: vm-multihoming
  annotations:
    k8s.v1.cni.cncf.io/networks: default/macvlan-network
spec:
  containers:
  - name: test-container
    image: registry.access.redhat.com/ubi8/ubi:latest
    command: ["/bin/bash"]
    args: ["-c", "sleep 3600"]
    resources:
      requests:
        memory: "64Mi"
        cpu: "50m"
EOF

# Apply the test pod
oc apply -f test-pod-macvlan.yaml
Wait for pods to be ready:
# Check pod status
oc get pods -n vm-multihoming

# Wait for pods to be running
oc wait --for=condition=Ready pod/test-pod-bridge -n vm-multihoming --timeout=300s
oc wait --for=condition=Ready pod/test-pod-macvlan -n vm-multihoming --timeout=300s
Subtask 3.3: Perform Connectivity Tests
Let's test network connectivity between the VM and test pods.

Get IP addresses of test pods:
# Get bridge network pod IP
oc exec test-pod-bridge -n vm-multihoming -- ip addr show

# Get macvlan network pod IP  
oc exec test-pod-macvlan -n vm-multihoming -- ip addr show
Test connectivity from VM to bridge network:
# Access VM console again
virtctl console fedora-multihome -n vm-multihoming

# Inside the VM, ping the bridge network gateway
ping -c 4 192.168.100.1

# Test connectivity to bridge network range
ping -c 4 192.168.100.10
Test connectivity from VM to macvlan network:
# Inside the VM, ping the macvlan network gateway
ping -c 4 192.168.200.1

# Test connectivity to macvlan network range
ping -c 4 192.168.200.10
Test connectivity from test pods to VM:
# Exit VM console (Ctrl+])

# Test from bridge network pod to VM
oc exec test-pod-bridge -n vm-multihoming -- ping -c 4 192.168.100.50

# Test from macvlan network pod to VM
oc exec test-pod-macvlan -n vm-multihoming -- ping -c 4 192.168.200.50
Subtask 3.4: Verify Network Interface Statistics
Let's examine network statistics and traffic flow.

Check network statistics on the VM:
# Access VM console
virtctl console fedora-multihome -n vm-multihoming

# Check interface statistics
cat /proc/net/dev

# Use netstat to check network connections
netstat -i

# Check ARP tables
arp -a
Monitor network traffic (optional):
# Install tcpdump if not already installed
sudo dnf install -y tcpdump

# Monitor traffic on eth1 (bridge network)
sudo tcpdump -i eth1 -c 10

# Monitor traffic on eth2 (macvlan network) 
sudo tcpdump -i eth2 -c 10
Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue 1: Network Attachment Definitions Not Found
Problem: Error message about network attachment definitions not being found.

Solution:

# Verify network attachment definitions exist
oc get network-attachment-definitions -A

# Check if they're in the correct namespace
oc get network-attachment-definitions -n default

# Recreate if missing
oc apply -f bridge-network.yaml
oc apply -f macvlan-network.yaml
Issue 2: VM Not Getting Additional IP Addresses
Problem: VM only shows the default interface.

Solution:

# Check VM annotations
oc get vmi fedora-multihome -o yaml | grep -A 10 annotations

# Verify Multus is working
oc get pods -n openshift-multus

# Check VM events for errors
oc describe vmi fedora-multihome
Issue 3: Network Connectivity Issues
Problem: Cannot ping between networks.

Solution:

# Check interface configuration inside VM
ip addr show
ip route show

# Verify network policies aren't blocking traffic
oc get networkpolicy -A

# Check if interfaces are up
ip link show
Issue 4: DataVolume Creation Fails
Problem: DataVolume stuck in pending state.

Solution:

# Check storage class availability
oc get storageclass

# Check PVC status
oc get pvc

# Use a different storage class if needed
# Edit the DataVolume YAML and change storageClassName
